  "explanation": "The proposal outlines an interesting integration of latent probing for multi-agent idea refinement on consumer hardware (12GB GPU). However, running multi-turn loops with an 8B model alongside offline hidden-state extraction, linear probe training, and structured prompt fallback interventions within a tight 6-week window presents considerable engineering and computational overhead.",
  "problems": [
    "Extracting and storing hidden states from an 8B model across multiple dialogue turns while maintaining the model quantized in a 12GB VRAM budget creates memory fragmentation and context-switching bottlenecks.",
    "Training a robust linear probe on pre-extracted hidden states requires a well-curated dataset that must be gathered and annotated within the limited 6-week project schedule."
  ],
  "improvements": [
    "Restrict the evaluation to single-turn critic probing rather than full multi-turn dialogue loops to conserve VRAM and simplify the debugging pipeline.",
    "Utilize already pre-trained probes or frozen feature extractors if available to bypass the offline training bottleneck."
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "passage_id": "s_5b01ecf103c7c8a53854",
      "claim": "Probing latent novelty judgments from hidden states can be used to condition responses.",
      "relation": "supports"
    },
    {
      "passage_id": "s_71a8caa72cd02af55734",
      "claim": "Probing mechanisms improve novelty judgment performance and mitigate medium novelty bias.",
      "relation": "supports"
    }
  ],
  "uncertainty": "The feasibility depends heavily on whether hidden state extraction and quantization frameworks can coexist smoothly within a single 12GB consumer GPU without exceeding memory limits during multi-turn generation."
}
